UniMath
005.1 Programming‑Languages
510 Mathematical‑Logic proof‑assistant material
============================================================
UniMath is a formalized‑mathematics library built on the Coq
proof assistant, grounded in univalent foundations and 
homotopy type theory (HoTT). It provides a constructive, 
equivalence‑centric environment for developing machine‑
verified mathematics with strict foundational discipline.

UniMath — Univalent Foundations Library for Coq

CLASS
Univalent Foundations / Homotopy Type Theory (HoTT)

SHAPE
Equivalence‑centric, intensional, constructive, categorical.

KEY FEATURES

Univalent
: equality behaves like equivalence; structures identified 
  up to iso.
Constructive
: proofs correspond to programs; no classical axioms.
Intensional
: identity types reflect computational structure.
Categorical
: category theory is the backbone of most constructions.
Modular
: library split into coherent packages with strict 
  boundaries.
Total
: proofs and constructions must be computationally 
  meaningful.

STRENGTHS

Strong categorical infrastructure.
Clean constructive foundations.
Machine‑checked mathematics.
Modular library design.
Ideal for equivalence‑respecting mathematics.
Supports large‑scale formalization projects.

============================================================
UNIVALENT FOUNDATIONS

Univalence: equivalences between types are equalities.
This allows mathematics to be developed up to isomorphism
rather than up to strict syntactic equality.

Equality = equivalence.
Structures identified up to iso.
Eliminates many extensionality headaches.
Central axiom of UniMath.

Consequences:
  Category theory becomes natural.
  Algebraic structures behave well under equivalence.
  Proofs become modular and transportable.

============================================================
CONSTRUCTIVE MATHEMATICS

UniMath is fully constructive:
No excluded middle.
No choice principles unless explicitly added.
Proofs correspond to computational content.
All constructions must be executable.

This aligns UniMath with intuitionistic logic and 
computational semantics.

============================================================
CATEGORY THEORY CORE

UniMath’s largest and most mature component is its category 
theory library.

Categories, functors, natural transformations.
Limits, colimits, adjunctions, monads.
Higher categorical structures.
Constructive versions of classical results.

Category theory is not an add‑on — it is the structural
backbone of UniMath.

============================================================
MODULAR LIBRARY DESIGN

Packages include:

Foundations
MoreFoundations
CategoryTheory
Algebra
NumberSystems
Topology
Combinatorics
K-theory (experimental)

Each package is designed to be independent and composable.

============================================================
INTENSIONAL TYPE THEORY

UniMath inherits Coq’s intensional equality:
Identity types reflect computation.
Extensionality must be added explicitly.
Univalence mitigates intensional friction but does not 
eliminate it.

This shapes how equality, equivalence, and transport behave.

============================================================
COQ‑BASED PROOF DEVELOPMENT

UniMath uses Coq as its proof engine:

  Tactics for constructive reasoning.
  Strict termination and definitional equality.
  Proofs are machine‑checked.

No general dependent pattern matching (restricted for safety).

============================================================
COMMON USE CASES

Formalization of category theory.
Constructive algebra and number systems.
Homotopy‑type‑theoretic mathematics.
Machine‑verified proofs.
Research in univalent foundations.

Development of equivalence‑respecting mathematical 
frameworks.

============================================================
BASIC EXAMPLE

A tiny UniMath‑style snippet (Coq):

---
Require Import UniMath.Foundations.All.

Definition nat_plus (m n : nat) : nat := m + n.

Lemma nat_plus_comm (m n : nat) :
  nat_plus m n = nat_plus n m.
Proof.
  apply Nat.add_comm.
Qed.
---

This is trivial, but illustrates the UniMath style:

  explicit imports,
  constructive definitions,

proofs via Coq’s intensional equality.

============================================================
STRUCTURAL & PHILOSOPHICAL LIMITS

UniMath is powerful, but its foundational shape is not 
neutral. It inherits deep structural constraints from:

  intensional type theory
  constructive logic
  univalence
  categorical primacy
  Coq’s termination discipline

These shape what mathematics feels natural — and what feels 
distorted.

EQUIVALENCE‑CENTRIC FOUNDATIONS
UniMath assumes:

  equivalence is fundamental
  equality is intensional
  structures are identified up to iso
  transport is central

Consequences:

  extensional equality is non‑primitive
  tuple‑heavy mathematics becomes awkward
  reasoning must be encoded categorically

RELATIONS ARE NON‑PRIMITIVE
Relations are encoded as:

  predicates
  dependent functions
  categorical morphisms

This makes:

  equivalence relations
  partial orders
  semantics
  multi‑agent systems
  graph theory

feel heavier than in relational foundations.

TUPLES ARE SECOND‑CLASS
UniMath uses:

  Σ‑types
  records
  dependent pairs

But tuples are not primitive:

  multi‑arity structures become nested
  projections become verbose
  constraints become deeply dependent

INTENSIONAL EQUALITY LIMITS EXTENSIONAL REASONING
Even with univalence:

  extensional equality is not default
  proofs require transport machinery
  identical structures may not be equal definitionally

This is a core friction point.

TOTALITY & CONSTRUCTIVITY SHAPE EXPRESSIVENESS
Because UniMath is constructive:

  partiality is disallowed
  classical reasoning must be encoded
  some theorems become unprovable
  some structures become unnatural to encode

This is foundational, not incidental.

PROOFS CAN BE BRITTLE
Because everything is encoded via:

  dependent types
  equivalences
  transports
  categorical morphisms

Small changes can break large proof chains.

NOT IDEAL FOR SEMANTIC DOMAINS
UniMath is awkward for:

  operational semantics
  denotational semantics
  multi‑agent systems
  graph theory
  database theory
  constraint systems

These domains want:

  primitive tuples
  extensional equality
  partiality
  multi‑arity constructs
  
UniMath’s foundations resist these.

PHILOSOPHICAL MISALIGNMENT

UniMath assumes:

  equivalence is primary
  equality is intensional
  computation is constructive
  category theory is central
  termination is mandatory

But many mathematical domains assume:

  extensional equality
  primitive tuples

============================================================
SUMMARY

UniMath is a powerful, rigorous environment for:

  constructive mathematics
  category theory
  univalent foundations
  homotopy type theory
  machine‑verified proofs

Its strengths come from:

  equivalence‑centric foundations
  categorical structure
  constructive logic
  modular library design

Its limitations come from:

  intensional equality
  lack of primitive relations
  tuple‑secondary foundations
  constructive totality
  categorical primacy

UniMath shape is univalent, constructive, intensional, and 
categorical. This shape is powerful for some domains and 
limiting for others. UniMath adds additional constraints:

  no general dependent pattern matching
  no classical axioms
  strict constructive discipline
  equivalence‑centric foundations
  categorical primacy

This pushes UniMath even further away from tuple‑centric
foundations.

